1
00:00:01,100 --> 00:00:03,604
This network has never seen this sentence.

2
00:00:03,904 --> 00:00:07,468
Pandas: the bear, or the Python library?

3
00:00:07,468 --> 00:00:11,213
Nobody told it which.
Yet it's almost certain: animal.

4
00:00:11,613 --> 00:00:15,163
Stranger still:
the word pandas barely matters to it.

5
00:00:15,463 --> 00:00:17,775
By the end, you'll know exactly why.

6
00:00:20,875 --> 00:00:22,888
What does a network actually read?

7
00:00:23,688 --> 00:00:29,437
Last time, a neuron asked two questions:
is python in the sentence? Is pandas?

8
00:00:29,537 --> 00:00:32,433
Real language needs a question
for every word

9
00:00:32,433 --> 00:00:34,964
in its dictionary: say 50,000.

10
00:00:35,764 --> 00:00:39,748
One thing we can do is put the sentence
into a bag and shake it.

11
00:00:39,748 --> 00:00:43,559
The order is gone;
only which words are inside remains.

12
00:00:43,709 --> 00:00:45,739
Mark those words in the dictionary,

13
00:00:45,739 --> 00:00:47,287
where every tick is one word,

14
00:00:47,287 --> 00:00:49,071
and that's a bag of words.

15
00:00:49,771 --> 00:00:52,130
A neuron reads the bag like a vote.

16
00:00:52,130 --> 00:00:54,316
For now, we set the votes by hand:

17
00:00:54,316 --> 00:00:56,714
snake votes strongly for animal,

18
00:00:56,714 --> 00:01:01,201
import votes against,
and the word "the" barely votes.

19
00:01:01,301 --> 00:01:03,556
Words not in the bag stay quiet.

20
00:01:03,556 --> 00:01:06,578
If the votes clear the bar,
it fires: animal.

21
00:01:07,310 --> 00:01:09,238
So what can you build out of votes?

22
00:01:09,938 --> 00:01:13,061
Votes and a bar make a tiny
programming language.

23
00:01:13,211 --> 00:01:17,509
Low bar: any animal word fires it.
That's the OR gate.

24
00:01:17,659 --> 00:01:21,873
High bar: it needs python and pandas.
That's the AND gate.

25
00:01:22,573 --> 00:01:24,863
A vote against gives you the NOT gate.

26
00:01:25,013 --> 00:01:28,248
Chain them,
like last lesson's software-topic neuron,

27
00:01:28,248 --> 00:01:31,534
and you have OR, AND, NOT gates:

28
00:01:31,534 --> 00:01:33,691
what every computer is built from.

29
00:01:33,791 --> 00:01:37,169
So neurons can write any rule
about which words appear.

30
00:01:37,469 --> 00:01:40,110
And one recipe always works: a list.

31
00:01:40,110 --> 00:01:42,418
One neuron per sentence you've seen,

32
00:01:42,418 --> 00:01:45,243
each saying yes to exactly that bag.

33
00:01:45,393 --> 00:01:48,814
Your turn: a new sentence arrives.
What does the list say?

34
00:01:51,114 --> 00:01:53,853
Nothing.
A list only knows what it has seen.

35
00:01:54,253 --> 00:01:56,154
And a row for every possible bag?

36
00:01:56,154 --> 00:01:59,018
Just writing down
how many fills this wall.

37
00:01:59,746 --> 00:02:02,698
Then how does it handle
a sentence it's never seen?

38
00:02:03,398 --> 00:02:06,755
By storing a vote per word,
not an answer per sentence.

39
00:02:06,905 --> 00:02:10,156
Watch the reach.
A list row covers one sentence.

40
00:02:10,256 --> 00:02:12,898
Take a word rule: "zoo votes animal".

41
00:02:12,898 --> 00:02:14,986
It covers every sentence with zoo,

42
00:02:14,986 --> 00:02:16,999
even ones it has never seen.

43
00:02:16,999 --> 00:02:19,162
Rules are cheaper than lists.

44
00:02:20,162 --> 00:02:21,406
So who sets the votes?

45
00:02:21,406 --> 00:02:24,458
A network can do it from labeled examples.

46
00:02:24,608 --> 00:02:29,415
Let's look at one example it gets wrong
during training: a snake hid in the woods.

47
00:02:29,415 --> 00:02:31,934
It should say animal, but it isn't sure.

48
00:02:32,034 --> 00:02:34,824
To fix it, the words in this sentence
get the blame:

49
00:02:34,824 --> 00:02:38,143
each one's vote is nudged a little
toward animal.

50
00:02:38,543 --> 00:02:40,116
Repeat thousands of times.

51
00:02:40,116 --> 00:02:43,115
Every nudge to zoo helps
every sentence with zoo,

52
00:02:43,115 --> 00:02:46,274
so the votes harden
into rules on their own.

53
00:02:46,424 --> 00:02:49,926
Animal words vote animal,
software words vote software,

54
00:02:49,926 --> 00:02:54,736
and words that show up everywhere,
like "the", barely vote.

55
00:02:54,936 --> 00:02:57,947
Your turn:
pandas shows up in animal sentences

56
00:02:57,947 --> 00:03:01,792
and software sentences alike.
What happens to its vote?

57
00:03:04,092 --> 00:03:06,386
It cancels out.
Each side pulls it back,

58
00:03:06,386 --> 00:03:08,601
so the words around it decide.

59
00:03:09,216 --> 00:03:13,163
Add two hidden neurons,
and blame flows back to them too:

60
00:03:13,163 --> 00:03:17,669
that's backpropagation,
the piece missing in 1969.

61
00:03:17,669 --> 00:03:20,898
Here, one learns animal words,
and the other becomes

62
00:03:20,898 --> 00:03:24,126
a software-topic neuron,
like last lesson's.

63
00:03:24,126 --> 00:03:26,379
This time, nobody wired it.

64
00:03:26,579 --> 00:03:31,856
The last neuron just weighs their votes:
animal votes yes, software votes no.

65
00:03:32,156 --> 00:03:33,978
Now: the pandas escaped the zoo.

66
00:03:33,978 --> 00:03:37,819
Zoo and escaped bring votes learned
from other sentences,

67
00:03:37,819 --> 00:03:40,295
and pandas cancels out: animal.

68
00:03:41,795 --> 00:03:46,475
Each word's two votes are its profile:
how much it leans toward each concept.

69
00:03:46,625 --> 00:03:50,469
Tiger and owl never shared a sentence,
yet their profiles match.

70
00:03:50,469 --> 00:03:54,694
Next, a closer look at word profiles:
how they work in practice,

71
00:03:54,694 --> 00:03:59,220
and why they're far more powerful
than the simple version we built here.
